760: #  THE CONTROL ARM TESTS BITS OF idata, so the 2-byte write cell owes each tested bit written both clear and set (the
761: #  generating-set clause; the Opus seat, F2: `relative_addr != CRIME_CONTROL || !(idata & CRIME_CONTROL_DOG_ENABLE)'
762: #  passed the 71-row version because no refused row carried DOG_ENABLE).  sh08 above writes HARD_RESET set / DOG clear;
763: #  here 0x0201 writes HARD_RESET clear / DOG set / bit 0 (the arm would print its unimplemented-control text) and 0x0a00
764: #  writes both set (the arm would stop every CPU).  A 1-byte write cannot carry either bit.
765: A = Addr(CODE)
766: x, _ = st(A, "sh", CRIME + 0x08, 0x0201)
767: e, _ = st(A, "sh", CRIME + 0x08, 0x0900)       # glm, closing pass: ENDIANESS (0x0100) -- CLEARED by the arm, never
768:                                                # branched on (this said "the arm's third tested bit" until repair 12)
769: y, _ = st(A, "sh", CRIME + 0x08, 0x0a00)
770: f, _ = st(A, "sh", CRIME + 0x08, 0xffff)        # all-ones: every bit a data-keyed narrowing could key on, set
771: #  and a follow-up refusal at ANOTHER label (the codex seat, second closing pass: a latch whose bit is keyed on one label,
772: #  `CRIME_W_LEN << (relative_addr == L)', is invisible to a session that refuses only at L)
773: g, _ = ld(A, "lb", CRIME + 0x07, "t1")
