801: 
802: # ---- the INTSTAT / INTMASK labels: their arm calls crime_interrupt_reassert(), which is NOT idempotent -- ----
803: #  INTMASK = bit 28 and SOFTINT = bit 28 are stored with 8-byte writes; SOFTINT's store goes to the default arm and does
804: #  NOT reassert, so the CPU's IP2 (cause bit 0x400) stays low until something reasserts.  A refused access at the label
805: #  must leave it low; the accepted `lw' that follows is the CONTROL that the arm reasserts at once.  (Measured by the
806: #  pass-2 Opus seat, opus_pass2_20260913/scripts/crime_p2_escape.py E1/E2: a fall-through at the label sets IP2.)
807: #  BIT carries bit 0 as well (the kimi CLI seat, second closing pass): a refused read answers the low bytes of whatever
808: #  the decline hands the tail, so with a register whose low bytes were 0 a decline that answered THE REGISTER
809: #  (`odata = d->reg[relative_addr / 8]' at INTSTAT or INTMASK) read back 0 and passed.  AND in its TOP lanes (the Opus
810: #  second-closing seat, measured 121/121 on the 0x10000001 seed): a decline that answers the bytes the ADDRESS names --
811: #  `odata = d->reg[relative_addr / 8] >> (8 * (8 - len - (relative_addr & 7)))', the lane the model's own 4-byte read
812: #  path uses -- reads bytes 0-1 at the label, which the 0x10000001 seed left 0.  So the seed is nonzero in byte 0, byte 1
813: #  and the low byte; INTSTAT = SOFTINT | HARDINT and INTMASK both carry it; the reassert still sees bit 28.
814: #  And glm (third closing pass) found a THIRD byte selection -- the 4-byte path's half, `reg >> 32' -- whose low bytes
815: #  that seed left 0.  So the seed is now nonzero in EVERY byte: no selection of the register's bytes can answer 0.  (DOG,
816: #  the far slot and the middle slot are seeded every-byte-nonzero already.)
817: BIT = 0x0101010111010101
