58: Derivations for the new rows (the first eighteen legs' other reds are carried from table 14):
59:   rearm ctl msg   the fixed build prints for 0x0201 and 0x0001 at both widths (4).  D_nodogclear and E_noendianclear add
60:                   the 0x0200 and 0x0100 texts (6); L_dogclr adds the 0x0200 texts only here, where the latch is SET (6);
61:                   W_ctl8w DECLINES the two latch-SET 8-byte writes that print (2).  Every refusal in this session is at
62:                   +0x06 (the default arm), so no decline-suppression leg reaches the CONTROL arm (M1 included).
63:   latch ctl msg   the same ten writes, latch CLEAR: D_nodogclear and E_noendianclear -> 6; L_dogclr, W_ctl8w keyed on
64:                   the latch SET -> 4.
65:   rst 4w/8w msg   twin 0x0001 (latch CLEAR, one text), refusal, then 0x800 (latch SET, residue 0 on the fixed build):
66:                   H_noresetclear and L_rstclr add the 0x800 text (2).  K_ctl4w/8w_reset_post and W_ctl8w DECLINE the
67:                   0x800 (their rst 4w/8w reds, as in table 14) and the count stays at the twin's 1.
68:   ctlrst msg      latch CLEAR throughout: H_noresetclear -> 2; nothing else reaches it (no refusal; no DOG or
69:                   ENDIANESS bit; the latch-keyed legs key on SET).
70:   ctlrst halt     S_rst8clr skips the 8-byte stop with the latch CLEAR: `ctlrst sd halt' reads 0x12345678; `rst 8w'
71:                   (latch SET) and `sw08 halts' (4-byte) stay green.
72:   W_istlb/W_imklb the only 1-byte READS at +0x10/+0x18 are each session's first refusal (latch CLEAR, the mutant still
73:                   declines): no row reads the cell.  Measured here so the adjudicators rule on a measurement.
