54: ### round's own table 10 refutes it.
55: 
56: Derived, not asserted: `scripts2/session_map2.py` replays the shipped probe with `session()` and `row()` intercepted
57: and decodes each session's command list back into `(op, offset, pc)` triples.  It does not run the emulator.  Output:
58: **45 sessions, 72 accepted CRIME accesses issued AFTER the session's first refusal**, in eleven sessions, and **ten of
59: those carry a VALUE or EFFECT oracle**:
60: 
61: ```
62: #18 imask   POST lw+0x18 ld+0x18   rows: imask ctl (IP2), imask keeps (value)
63: #19 istat   POST lw+0x10 ld+0x18   rows: istat ctl (IP2)
64: #21-24 time POST ld+0x38 x2 each   rows: time lb/sb/lh/sh ctl (TIME moved)
65: #25 keeps   POST ld+0x30 x2, sd+0x40, ld+0x40   rows: sb keeps, sh keeps, lo keeps
66: #26 rearm   POST all 46 ACCEPTS    rows: rearm once, rearm surv  -- NOTICE COUNT ONLY
67: #27 top     POST ld/lw+0x278, lw/sw+0x27c, sw+0x278, ld+0x278   rows: top8 st+ld, top4 rhi/rlo/w
68: #28 hi      POST ld+0x278          rows: hi keeps
69: #29 mid     POST ld+0x1f8, sd+0x1f8  rows: mid keeps
70: #30 dog     POST sd+0x38           (seed only -- no oracle in the useful direction)
71: #40 latch x5  firstRefusal at index 46: its 46 ACCEPTS run BEFORE the lh, POST = 0
72: ```
73: 
74: **The round's own kill table 10 is the measurement that settles it**: `W_8r` -- the same `d->warned`-keyed shrink at
75: the WIDTH level -- scored **145/157 and reddened twelve rows**, all of them post-latch value rows
76: (`killtable10_20260915/PRETABLE.out`, `POSTTABLE.out`).  That is impossible if every value row were refusal-free.
77: 
78: **Why the correction changes the repair rather than only the record:** the closure is **not** 46 cells.  Eight of the
79: 46 are already pinned post-latch (INTSTAT 4R, INTMASK 4R and 8R, TIME 8R, DOG 8R, default 8W and 8R at +0x40, default
80: 8R at +0x278).  The open set is smaller and nameable -- see S4.
81: 
82: ### CORRECTION 2 -- attribution of the TIME finding.
83: 
84: The harvest credits codex and says *"grok and kimi-k3 called it equivalent"*.  Both halves check out
85: (`grok.txt:42` tabulates TIME 4W/4R as **equivalent**; `ollama_kimi.txt:11` reasons from G3's 156 that it is
86: equivalent).  **It omits minimax, which also called the equivalence wrong** -- in its verdict line, independently.
87: Its *conclusion* is right and its *mechanism* is wrong: it argues from a "low lane" at +0x38, but at +0x38
88: `relative_addr & 4` is 0, so a 4-byte access there is the HIGH lane; the low lane is +0x3c, which is not a case label
89: and not in `ACCEPTS`.  Count the arguments, not the seats: **one seat gave the right reason (codex), one reached the
90: right verdict by a wrong route (minimax), two ratified the wrong claim (grok, kimi-k3).**
91: 
92: ### CORRECTION 3 -- F4b's attribution, and two refuted seat claims.
93: 
94: F4b refutes **deepseek**, not "kimi-k3's query": deepseek's floor-gap claim was
95: `if (len == 4 && relative_addr == CRIME_INTMASK && writeflag == MEM_READ) goto crime_declined;` (its `ollama_deepseek.txt`
96: item 3) -- that is F4a verbatim, **measured 155/157, CAUGHT** -- and its companion guess that INTSTAT 4R is *"likely
97: equivalent"* is refuted by F4b at 156/157 {istat ctl}: the channel is the REASSERT, not the value.  kimi-k3 named
98: INTSTAT 4R too and got it RIGHT (*"in fact pinned by an incidental row, but the mapping never says so"*); crediting the
99: kill to it inverts who was corrected.  Minor in the harvest, load-bearing in a ledger entry.
100: 
101: ---
102: 
103: ## 2.  THE FIND, PINNED
104: 
105: ### (a) HOW GENERAL THE CLASS IS -- derived from the handler, cell by cell
106: 
107: A silent decline at an accepted cell removes exactly three things: the `d->reg[]` update (writes), the `odata` it would
108: have answered (reads), and the switch arm's side effect.  Working those against the function
109: (`dev_sgi_ip32.c:229-231` prelude, `:275-320` width arms, `:322-409` switch, `:411-420` tail) gives, over the 46
110: `ACCEPTS` entries:
111: 
112: * **EQUIVALENT -- nothing observable at all (10 entries):** REV 8W / 4W-hi / 4R-hi out of the window (the prelude
113:   rewrites `reg[0]`, its high half is 0); CONTROL 4R-hi (high half of 0x100 is 0); CONTROL 4W and 8W at `0`, `0x0200`
114:   and `0x0100` (DOG is a local, ENDIANESS is cleared unconditionally, the leftover is 0 so nothing prints).
115: * **HOST-DIAGNOSTIC ONLY -- the guest cannot tell (5 entries):** CONTROL 4W and 8W at `0x0201` and `0x0001` (the
116:   leftover-nonzero `fatal()` line), and the default-arm 4W at `+0x04` (its `debug()` write line; the store itself lands
117:   in REV's low lane, which the prelude rewrites).
118: * **GUEST-VISIBLE (31 entries):** everything else.  The channels are the PROM-window redirect (`pc = ra`, the four
119:   window kinds), a nonzero register answer (REV 0x11, CONTROL 0x100, INTSTAT, INTMASK, DOG, TIME, the far and middle
120:   blocks), a durable store read back later (INTMASK, DOG, TIME, every default-arm slot), `crime_interrupt_reassert()`
121:   raising or lowering `cause.IP2` (INTSTAT and INTMASK, all four kinds each), and the skipped
122:   `crime_update_crime_time()` (TIME, all four kinds).
123: 
124: **Every one of the 31 is reachable in the K form**, because the mutant's condition is just `(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN)`
125: and any session that has refused once satisfies it.  So the class is not three cells; it is thirty-one.
126: 
127: ### (b) WHY NO SHIPPED ROW SEES IT -- and exactly which eight it does see
128: 
129: From S1's session map: of the 31 guest-visible cells, **eight are exercised post-latch with an oracle** and
130: **twenty-three are not**.  `rearm once` (#26) runs all 46 post-latch and is the reason the mutant is *reachable* in the
131: shipped probe -- and it counts notices only, so it is blind by construction.  `latch x5` (#40) runs its 46 BEFORE its
132: first refusal, so the K mutant is inert there and `latch said` stays green.
133: 
134: That is why K1/K2/K3 score 157/157 while a PRE/POST discriminator shows a guest difference at each
135: (`logs/disc_K*.out`): INTMASK 4W lost (`0x0` vs `0x1000000000000000`), the REV window redirect lost (pc `0xbfc05184`
136: vs ra), CONTROL 8R answering `0x0` vs `0x100` -- with the notice count 1 in every case.
137: 
