FILE regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\ctl0c_discharge_20260917\VERDICT.md
1: # `#529` L1 -- THE `ctl0c` PAIR IS **DISCHARGED**.  The rows stay.
2: 
3: ## THE MEASUREMENT (`RUN.out`, sentinel `KILLCTL0C_DONE 2026-09-17 12:53:02`)
4: 
5:     base              CRIMEP_RESULT=204/204
6:     M_ctl0c_decline   CRIMEP_RESULT=202/204
7: 
8:     acc ctl0c     base ok -> mutant FAIL
9:     p acc ctl0c   base ok -> mutant FAIL
10:     acc ctl4r     base ok -> mutant UNMOVED
11:     p acc ctl4r   base ok -> mutant UNMOVED
12: 
13: **THE PREDICTION WAS WRITTEN INTO `mutants_ctl0c.py` BEFORE THE RUN, so its own mtime proves the
14: order**, and it read: *"`acc ctl0c` and `p acc ctl0c` RED ... `acc ctl4r` / `p acc ctl4r` UNMOVED
15: ... EXPECTED SCORE 202/204."*  **Exact match on all four rows and on the score.**
16: 
17: ## CONTROLS, ALL PASSED
18: 
19:     PROBE FROZEN cmp=clean                      the instrument is the shipped one
20:     CLONE START: dev_sgi_ip32.c cmp CLEAN       baseline identity before mutant 1
21:     binary DIFFERS from base                    *** the edit is LIVE IN THE OBJECT ***
22:     CLONE RESTORED cmp=clean binary-nt-source=yes
23: 
24: That third control is the one that makes 202/204 a result: a mutant that never applied scores
25: exactly like a clean sweep, and this project has a standing rule that the base/mutant binary `cmp`
26: is what separates them.
27: 
28: ## *** THE ROWS WERE NEVER VACUOUS.  THE MUTANT SET WAS INCOMPLETE. ***
29: 
30: The finding that opened this was *"`acc ctl0c` NEVER REDS under any of the FIFTEEN LEGS of table
31: 12"*, and the honest reading of that was: either the rows are vacuous by construction, or fifteen
32: legs all missed the shape that moves them.  **It is the second.**
33: 
34: `acc ctl0c` reads `lw +0x0c` and wants `0x100`.  That value is produced by the **WIDTH ARM**
35: (`} else if (len == 4) { ... odata = d->reg[relative_addr / 8]`, then `if (relative_addr & 4) odata
36: = (int32_t)odata`), which runs **BEFORE the register switch**.  `+0x0c` has no case label, so it
37: lands in `default:` -- a debug print only.
38: 
39: > **SO A DECLINE AT `default:` CANNOT MOVE THIS ROW, AND NEITHER CAN `G_clr_ctl4r`'s
40: > `relative_addr == CRIME_CONTROL` (0x08), WHICH NEVER TOUCHES 0x0c.**  Every one of the fifteen
41: > legs was aimed where this row's value does not come from.
42: 
43: The discharging mutant therefore has to decline **before** the width arm:
44: 
45:     if (relative_addr == 0x0c) goto crime_declined;
46: 
47: which is the clause-(d) shape -- keep the guard, reroute its effect -- and is a plausible future
48: edit: someone deciding this device should REFUSE unimplemented offsets rather than answer them out
49: of the width arm.
50: 
51: ## DISPOSITION
52: 
53: **Accept-row failability is satisfied: the pair is discharged BY A MEASURED KILL, not by existing.**
54: The Fable adjudication's L1 obligation -- *"discharge them or remove them, BEFORE #529 commits"* --
55: is met by the first branch.  **The rows stay, and `#529`'s L1 is closed.**
56: 
57: *Worth keeping for the next table: the reason fifteen legs missed this is not carelessness but
58: AIM.  A row whose value is produced before the switch cannot be moved by any mutant that edits the
59: switch, however many of them you build.*

