5: **VERDICT: DO NOT CLOSE. Repair 15's rows and table 14 are sound and exact, but one DERIVED floor escape (delete `idata &= ~CRIME_CONTROL_HARD_RESET;`) is graded L2 like its DOG twin — build it, add the pair in-round, pass-2 the repair; the ENDIANESS clear is clause (e), not L2; codex is walled and owes a re-fire.**
6: 
7: ## Q1 — premises
8: - **(a) TRUE.** Fresh `pty.fork`+`os.execv` per session; the shared inputs are an immutable 16-byte `/tmp/dummy_crimep.bin` and the host clock, and the clock only feeds a within-session delta against a per-session `last_microseconds` (load-sensitive, disclosed, fails red not green). `-V -x` semantics not read from source; non-load-bearing given process isolation.
9: - **(b) TRUE, now verified to the instruction.** `machine.c:710 run_instr` precedes the tick loop at `:726`, so in the first `step` the s0 `ld` latches `last_microseconds` (adds nothing), then the one tick adds the µs since — 66–286 in every log. The len==8 read takes `odata` *before* the switch's `crime_update_crime_time`, so s1 reads the tick's value; both refusals `goto` past TIME on the fix. M3 exempts the latch-SET refusal → s1 ≈ 2.8e8. Sound.
10: - **(c) TRUE.** `case CRIME_REV` tests only the pc window — not `writeflag`, not `len` — so `lb/lh/sb/sh` at relative_addr 0 all redirect once the goto is skipped; M5 reds all four `rl … pc`, base reads `0xbfc05184`.
11: - **(d) TRUE as scoped, and the scope is the trap.** For the 0x0200 cell the three shapes are right (residue-print → D_nodogclear; DOG-as-stop → `rearm/latch surv`, which write 0x0200 then `roundtrip`; re-arm → `rearm once`/`latch x5`). But DOG is not the only tested bit with a "clear so the residue stays quiet" repair — HARD_RESET has the same one, unrowed (Q2).
12: - **(e) TRUE for the decline-suppression class.** REV/CONTROL/INTSTAT/INTMASK/TIME are the guest-observable arms; DOG is `break;`, `default` is host `debug()` (and rowed anyway by `nodflt`); mirrors 0x80–0x1ff are not redirected in code and fall to `default`; the prelude's `exit(1)` paths are construction invariants, inert at `-M 64`. The gap is on the accepted-CONTROL residue axis, not the label axis.
13: 
14: ## Q2 — the floor on the composite
15: Decline-suppression {observable label × latch SET}: **5/5 carried** (M3 TIME 214, M5 REV 214, M6 INTSTAT 215, M7 INTMASK 215, M8 CONTROL 215; M1 reds all 18 latch-SET cells and, verified, no first-refusal row). Latch-CLEAR TIME carried by M4. Swept and clean.
16: 
17: **The miss — I agree with the dedicated seat.** Delete the HARD_RESET branch's `idata &= ~CRIME_CONTROL_HARD_RESET;`: the `running = 0` loop precedes it, so an accepted `0x800` still halts; `idata` stays `0x800`, the ENDIANESS mask does not touch it, `idata != 0` prints, `fatal()` returns. The only sessions writing an accepted `0x800` are `sw08 halts`, `rst 4w`, `rst 8w` (frozen probe line 1592 is the sole site); they read `v1 == SENT` (still true) and count `-- declined.` (unchanged); the six CONTROL-text rows (lines 648/704/722/940/1332/1464) hold no `0x800`; no control row moves. **All 218 green by derivation.** The probe's HARD_RESET disclosure (line 421, "nothing can follow it … unrowable") is about a *re-arm*, whose observable needs a later access; the residue print is emitted *during* the store and lands in the transcript, so this cell is rowable and simply unrowed.
18: 
19: **Grade: L2, the same as the DOG pair — not disclosed.** HARD_RESET is tested as a case (`if (idata & HARD_RESET)`), so it is in the CONTROL arm's generating set; the clear is that branch's repair; nulling a tested partition's repair is the canonical (d) mutant, and the round already graded the identical shape on DOG "L2, IN as the pair." Grading the twin lower would be the #499 two-of-a-two-by-two. Owed in-round, **test-first**: build D_noresetclear and show 218/218; add `ctlreset sw msg` / `ctlreset sd msg` (accepted `0x800` at +0x08 as the session's last access, count `CRIME_CONTROL: unimplemented` == 0, the halt `v1 == SENT` as the in-session liveness half so a dead channel cannot read 0 for free); show the pair reds the mutant, greens the fix, moves no other leg. Then close the class in the contract: each *tested* CONTROL bit carries a side-effect detector and a residue-print detector (DOG: ctldog + surv; HARD_RESET: rst + ctlreset).
20: 
21: **ENDIANESS — clause (e), not L2.** `idata &= ~CRIME_CONTROL_ENDIANESS;` is unconditional; the arm never branches on the bit, so it is outside the tested generating set {DOG, HARD_RESET} — which is how the probe's contract and my 2026-09-25 re-ruling already grade it. Under the #549 pin (TESTS = masks-or-compares *as a case*), an untested bit crossed with the relational `idata != 0` compare is the two-axis joint a probe discloses; grading it by consequence (the print is real — PROM/NetBSD write ENDIANESS, and the prelude re-forces CONTROL = 0x100) would reopen #548. Two honest riders: the deletion **also escapes 218/218** (the 0x0100 ACCEPTS writes run only inside `rearm`/`latch x5`, which count `-- declined.`), so the disclosure must say *escapes, disclosed* and name the residue-deletion form, which today it does not; and a text count in one of those sessions is a cheap optional strengthening, not owed.
22: 
23: ## Q3 — the ctldog pair
24: **Sound.** Fix: 0x0200 clears DOG → residue 0 → silent; the 0x0201 twin prints once → count 1 (base logs 1/1). D_nodogclear: residue 0x200 prints → count 2 (D logs 2/2, FAIL). So the brief's question — can the row read ONE when 0x0200 *did* print — is answered by the mutant itself: it reads 2. The twin is a genuine in-session liveness half; a bare 0 cannot pass. The member neither the ruling nor the brief named is the HARD_RESET residue pair above.
25: 
26: ## Q4 — table 14 vs predictions
27: **Exact, 18/18.** check14.py compares FAILING sets, tallies, denominator 218 and CONTROL per leg, refuses logs without `CRIMEP_DONE`, and would have flagged a predicted-red 218/218 as ESCAPE. PRETABLE14 (00:33:06) precedes the run (00:33:17) and every log. The 14 new rows red only under their named killers (tl: M1/M3/M4; rl: M1/M5; ctldog: D). No miss to explain. What the table cannot show is a mutant nobody built — which is the whole reason the ask-again found it.
28: 
29: ## Where the colleague's draft is wrong or overstated
30: 1. **"MEASURED floor breach" / "airtight" — overstated.** Nobody has built or run D_noresetclear; the dedicated seat's find and the draft's confirmation are both walks. This project's own rule (the #549 M_HUHV case: "it would sweep the table" is a measurement, never an inference) makes it a *derived* escape, measurement owed. I checked the specific way that lesson bit last time — a row already driving the value — and found none, so I expect the build to confirm; "expect" is the word.
31: 2. **Q1(b)'s intra-step order was asserted without source.** It is correct — `run_instr` precedes the tick loop — but it had not been earned.
32: 3. **Roster statement is stale.** The draft cites POSTTABLE ("codex pending to 07:01"); the live panel record says codex **WALLED MID-ANSWER** (830 b, usage limit) and owes one hand re-fire before the round closes; the kimi CLI stalled at 284 b but is COVERED by kimi-k3:cloud. Either way the chain is not closed on roster grounds, and Astra must confirm before any commit.
33: 4. The draft does not answer the ENDIANESS question at all; it is answered above, and the grading line between HARD_RESET (tested → L2) and ENDIANESS (untested → clause e) is the same line the round drew for DOG vs ENDIANESS and the #549 pin drew for TxICR.
34: 5. Minor: "#499 shape *verbatim*" — analogous, not verbatim. The non-blocking note that the latch-CLEAR first-refusal rows at INTSTAT/INTMASK/REV are green on all 18 legs is a true measurement (I ran it) and correctly not a repair-15 breach.
