176: `idata = 0` in the branch) takes 0x0201's print away, which reds `p acc ctl msg` (4 -> 2) and both `ctldog` rows (1 -> 0).
177: That one is by reading; I did not build it. **"DOG treated as a stop bit"** is live only if it acts inside the DOG
178: branch or ahead of it; the widening PRETABLE14 gives as its example is dead code. A live stop at 0x0200 stops the CPU in
179: `rearm`/`latch x5` (`rearm surv`/`latch surv`), and in `ctldog` the 0x0201 twin then never runs (count 0).
180: 
181: **(e)** `switch (relative_addr)` names 0x00, 0x08, 0x10, 0x18, 0x30 and 0x38. A narrow access anywhere else lands in
182: `default:`, which is `debug()` only. `odata` is never written for a narrow access, so a fall-through read still answers
183: 0, and the v0 rows cannot see any suppression. The observable effects and their conditions:
184: 
185: * REV: pc = ra, only from 0xbfc051xx (`:344`).
186: * CONTROL: writes only (`:356`). HARD_RESET stops the CPUs; otherwise there is only host text for a nonzero residue.
187: * INTSTAT/INTMASK: `crime_interrupt_reassert()` is observable only when it CHANGES the line (`:118` raise, `:120` lower).
188:   In the census, every refused access at 0x10/0x18 ran with prev_asserted = 0. S18/S19 ran with SOFTINT = INTMASK =
189:   0x0101010111010101; the free-running S46/S47 ran with both at 0. So the raise is covered and **the lower is never
190:   exercised**. The discriminator shows that even the plain M6
191:   (= `P_ist_set`) lowers IP2 when the line is up. The rows kill M6 only through the raise.
192: * TIME: an update, visible at the next accepted read.
